        <div id="filter-vs-purifier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Filter vs Purifier: The Distinction That Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important thing to understand before buying is the difference between a filter and a purifier. In the outdoor industry, a filter removes bacteria, such as E. coli and salmonella, and protozoa, such as giardia and cryptosporidium. A purifier also removes viruses, such as norovirus and hepatitis A, which are far smaller and pass straight through most hollow-fiber membranes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Four of the five products here are filters. The Sawyer Squeeze, Katadyn BeFree, Platypus GravityWorks and LifeStraw Personal all use hollow-fiber membranes rated for bacteria and protozoa, not viruses. The GRAYL GeoPress is the one purifier, and its manufacturer rates it for viruses as well.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Use a filter for clear wild water. Use a purifier, or boil, when sewage or flood water could be involved.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">In North American backcountry water, protozoa and bacteria are the main risks and a filter covers them. Viruses come mainly from human waste, which is exactly what flood water and broken mains can carry.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Why does this matter at home? Because the water you are most likely to be filtering in an emergency is not a clear mountain stream. Flood water, water from a contaminated well and water during a boil notice can all carry viruses. In those cases, follow your local health authority's instructions. If they say boil, boil when you can. A purifier, or a filter followed by chemical treatment, is the backup when you cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of these devices remove dissolved salt, and most hollow-fiber filters do not reduce chemicals, fuel or heavy metals. Water that smells of petrol or chemicals, or that has been in contact with flood debris from industrial sites, is not made safe by any filter on this list. Use stored water instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Squeeze, Straw, Bottle and Gravity: Choosing a Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable filters come in four main designs, and the design decides how useful the filter is in practice. Squeeze filters, like the Sawyer, screw onto a pouch or bottle of dirty water and you squeeze clean water out. They are versatile: you can drink from them, fill a bottle or fill a cooking pot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Straw filters, like the LifeStraw Personal, are the simplest and cheapest, but you can only drink through them directly from the source. Bottle filters, like the Katadyn BeFree and GRAYL GeoPress, combine a container and a filter, so you fill and drink, but they hold a fixed volume at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity filters, like the Platypus GravityWorks, use a hanging bag of dirty water and let gravity push it through the filter into a clean bag. They take a little setting up, but they filter litres at a time while you get on with other things, which is what a family needs.
          </p>

