          <p>
            For most households, the best combination is a gravity filter for volume at home and a squeeze or straw filter for each bag or car. Add a purifier if your area floods or relies on well water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Water Planning for Outages and Boil Notices
          </h2>

          <p>
            The usual guidance for emergency water is at least one gallon per person per day, for several days, stored before you need it. Stored water is still the foundation. A filter does not replace it; it extends it, by letting you use other sources once stored water runs low.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about where that other water would come from. Homes on a well lose water when the power goes out unless the pump is on a <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">generator</a> or battery backup. Homes on city water may keep pressure but lose safety if a main breaks or a treatment plant is flooded. Rain barrels, pools, hot water tanks and nearby streams are all possible sources, each with different risks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water in your hot water tank is usually the cleanest emergency source in the house, because it came from the treated supply before the problem started. Pool and rain barrel water is fine to filter for washing and, with a purifier or boiling, for drinking in a real emergency. Flood water is the worst source of all and should be avoided for drinking even with a filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the filter with the rest of the outage supplies: <a href="/best-headlamp/">headlamps</a>, a <a href="/best-emergency-radio/">radio</a>, batteries and a <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">power bank</a>. A filter in a hiking bag in the garage is no help if you cannot find it in the dark.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flow Rate and Capacity: What the Numbers Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers dominate portable filter listings: flow rate and capacity. Flow rate tells you how quickly clean water comes out, usually in litres per minute when new. Capacity tells you how many litres the filter can treat before it needs replacing. Both are measured under ideal conditions, with clear water and a new filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            In real use, flow drops as the filter clogs with sediment. Clear water barely slows a hollow-fiber filter; muddy water can slow it to a trickle within a few litres. That is why backflushing or shaking matters: it pushes the trapped sediment back out and restores most of the flow. Filters that cannot be cleaned, like the GRAYL cartridge, slow down for good as they load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity figures for hollow-fiber filters are large, from hundreds to thousands of litres, and for most households the filter will outlast its pouches or bags. Purifier cartridges are rated for far less, often a few hundred uses, so budget for replacements if you choose a purifier for regular use.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single best thing you can do for any filter's flow and life is to pre-filter dirty water. Let it settle in a bucket and pour the clearer water off the top, or pour it through a clean cloth or coffee filter first. It takes a minute and can double the useful life of the filter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Storage, Freezing and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hollow-fiber filters have one serious weakness: freezing. Once a filter has been wet, water trapped in the fibers can expand as it freezes and crack them, leaving a filter that looks fine but lets bacteria through. If a wet filter may have frozen, the safe course is to replace it. In winter, keep it in an inside pocket or a warm room.
          </p>

          <p>
            New, dry filters can be stored for years. For a kit filter, the simplest approach is to leave it sealed in its packaging until you need it. For a filter you also use for camping, backflush it with clean water after each trip, let it dry fully, and store it in a sealed bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pouches and bags are the parts that wear out first. Squeeze pouches crack at the seams after heavy use, and gravity bags can puncture. Buy a spare pouch or bag for the kit, and inspect them before storm season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep dirty and clean sides separate. The quickest way to defeat a good filter is to let dirty water drip onto the clean outlet or the mouth of the clean bottle. Mark the dirty bag or pouch, keep the clean side capped, and wash your hands before handling the clean side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tap-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Can a Portable Filter Replace a Home Water Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, and it is worth being clear about why. Portable filters are designed to remove microorganisms from untreated water. Home filters, such as <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">pitcher filters</a>, under-sink systems and whole-house systems, are designed to reduce chlorine, taste, odour and specific contaminants such as lead from treated tap water. They solve different problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most hollow-fiber portable filters do little for chlorine taste, lead or PFAS, because those are dissolved in the water and pass straight through a membrane that is sized to catch organisms. The GRAYL is the partial exception, because GRAYL lists reduction of some chemicals and heavy metals, but it is still not a substitute for a certified home system for everyday use.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the right setup for most homes is both. A certified home filter, chosen from our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration guide</a>, for everyday drinking water, and a portable filter in the emergency kit for when the tap stops or the supply is compromised.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only have room in the budget for one emergency purchase, stored water comes first, then a gravity filter for the household, then a purifier if your area floods. Each step covers a scenario the one before it does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Portable Water Filter Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For one filter that covers most situations, buy the Sawyer Squeeze. It is small, cheap and long-lasting, and it can drink, fill bottles and fill pots. Add a spare pouch and it is ready for a kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a family, add the Platypus GravityWorks. Filtering eight litres at a time without effort is what makes a multi-day outage manageable.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you live somewhere that floods, rely on a well, or travel to places with uncertain water, buy the GRAYL GeoPress as well, because it is the only option here rated for viruses. And put a LifeStraw Personal in every car and go-bag as a cheap last resort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, keep it with your stored water and outage supplies, keep it from freezing once used, and follow your local health authority's advice during any boil notice.
          </p>
        </div>

