          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every filter removes viruses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most hollow-fiber filters are rated for bacteria and protozoa only. Viruses come mainly from human waste, which flood water and broken mains can carry. For that water, boil it, treat it chemically after filtering, or use a purifier rated for viruses.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting a used filter freeze</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water trapped in a wet hollow-fiber filter can crack the fibers as it freezes, leaving a filter that looks fine but lets bacteria through. Keep used filters warm in winter, and replace any wet filter that may have frozen.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filtering muddy water without pre-filtering</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sediment clogs a filter fast and shortens its life. Let dirty water settle in a bucket and pour off the clearer top, or pour it through a cloth first. A minute of preparation can double how much water a filter treats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a portable filter to fix tap water taste</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable filters target organisms, not chlorine, lead or other dissolved contaminants. For everyday tap water, a certified pitcher, faucet or under-sink filter does the job. Keep the portable filter for emergencies and untreated sources.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a filter as a substitute for stored water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A filter extends your stored water; it does not replace it. Store at least a gallon per person per day for several days, and use the filter once that runs low or for washing and cooking water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

