          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people the Sawyer Squeeze is the best all-round choice. Its 0.1-micron hollow fiber is rated for bacteria and protozoa, it threads onto pouches and ordinary bottles, it can be backflushed to restore flow, and it is small and inexpensive enough to keep in every emergency kit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do portable water filters remove viruses?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do not. Hollow-fiber filters like the Sawyer, Katadyn BeFree, Platypus GravityWorks and LifeStraw Personal are rated for bacteria and protozoa. A purifier, such as the GRAYL GeoPress, is rated for viruses too. When viruses are a risk, boil the water or use a purifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a portable water filter during a boil water notice?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow your local health authority's instructions first; if they say boil, boil. A standard filter is not rated for viruses, so it does not replace boiling. A purifier rated for viruses, or a filter followed by chemical treatment, is the backup when boiling is impossible.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable filter make flood water safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is not recommended. Flood water can carry viruses, chemicals, fuel and sewage, and no filter on this list removes all of those. Use stored water, bottled water or water from your hot water tank instead, and treat flood water as unsafe to drink.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a portable water filter last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hollow-fiber filters are rated for hundreds to thousands of litres, and usually outlast their pouches and bags with care. Purifier cartridges are rated for far less. Freezing, heavy sediment and poor cleaning shorten any filter's life, so pre-filter and backflush as recommended.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gravity filter better than a squeeze filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a family, a gravity filter is easier because it treats litres at a time without effort. For one person or a kit bag, a squeeze filter is smaller and more flexible. Many households keep a gravity filter at home and squeeze or straw filters in cars and go-bags.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do portable water filters remove chlorine or lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most hollow-fiber filters do not, because those contaminants are dissolved in the water and pass through a membrane sized for organisms. GRAYL lists reduction of some chemicals and heavy metals for the GeoPress. For everyday tap water, use a certified home water filter.
              </p>
            </div>

